    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All tests pass \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`, 16 tests, 0 failures.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `capacityWarning` flag following the existing `bulkSignupWarning` precedent exactly, keeping each concern in its own small unit and reusing the code that already counts a city's owners.\n\n**What changed:**\n\n1. **`AssignOwnerCapacityWarning.java`** (new) \u2014 a single-responsibility step that sets the flag when the city already holds 40\u201349 owners. It reuses `Cities.countIn(...)` (the same count set the hard rejection uses, so warning and rejection can never disagree) and references `EnsureCityUnderCapacity.CAPACITY` as the single source of truth for the limit of 50. The upper bound `< CAPACITY` means the flag is only about *approaching* \u2014 the hard 409 rejection at 50 is untouched.\n\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired `assignCapacityWarning` into the assign chain right after `assignBulkSignupWarning`, with `govern: [ transaction ]` like its neighbours. Placed after `build` and before `save`, so the count excludes the owner being created (it isn't persisted yet) \u2014 matching the \"already has\" semantics.\n\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the persisted `capacityWarning` boolean with `isCapacityWarning()`/`setCapacityWarning(...)`, capturing the warning at creation time (so a later `GET` returns the value as observed then, consistent with `bulkSignupWarning`).\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `capacityWarning` read-only boolean to the Owner schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gains the field and MapStruct maps it by name automatically.\n\n5. **DB schemas** (hsqldb, postgres, h2, mysql) \u2014 added the `capacity_warning BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT FALSE` column to match the non-null entity field.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreWarnsWhenApproachingCapacity` (fill a city to 40, next owner warns) passes, as does the full suite.",
